mn at at most one point, but not necessarily a point in T_n. So the maximum number of points covered by non-sunny lines in column c is V_c + H + D (if all horizontal and slope -1 lines intersect column c at points in T_n). So the total coverage capacity per column is at most k (sunny) + V_c + H + D.\n\nBut we have constraints: sum of V_c over all columns = V (since each vertical line is assigned to a column). So V_c is 1 for V columns, 0 for others.\n\nThus for each column c, we need h_c ≤ k + V_c + H + D. Since H + D = n - k - V. So h_c ≤ k + V_c + (n - k - V) = n - V + V_c. So h_c ≤ n - V + V_c. Since V_c ≤ 1, we have h_c ≤ n - V + 1 if column has a vertical line, else h_c ≤ n - V.\n\nNow h_c = n+1-c. So for column c with no vertical line, we need n+1-c ≤ n - V => 1 - c ≤ -V => c ≥ V+1. So columns with index c ≤ V must have vertical lines? Let's solve: For column c without vertical line, we need n+1-c ≤ n - V => subtract n: 1 - c ≤ -V => c ≥ V+1. So columns with c ≤ V cannot be without vertical line; they must have vertical lines. So we need at least V vertical lines covering columns 1,...,V. So V ≤ n (obviously). So we can assign vertical lines to the leftmost columns.\n\nNow consider column c = V+1 (the first column without vertical line). Then we need h_{V+1} = n+1-(V+1) = n - V ≤ n - V (since V_c=0). So equality holds. So column V+1 can be covered without vertical line, using only sunny, horizontal, and slope -1 lines. For column V+2, h_{V+2} = n - V - 1 ≤ n - V? Yes, it's less. So all columns beyond V+1 have h_c ≤ n - V, which is satisfied.\n\nThus the condition reduces to: we need V vertical lines covering columns 1,...,V. So we need at least V vertical lines, where V = number of vertical lines we choose. So any V from 0 to n is possible.\n\nNow we need to ensure that we have enough horizontal and slope -1 lines to cover the remaining points. Let's consider the total number of points to be covered by non-vertical lines (i.e., horizontal + slope -1 lines). Th